{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example 14 &middot; cookie consent</p>
    <h1>Roasted on Tuesday, in your cup by Friday</h1>
    <p>
      We buy three or four coffees at a time, roast them in twelve-kilo batches on the
      roastery floor, and pour them in the cafe beside the drum. Come and watch.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    <article class="card">
      <h2>The cafe</h2>
      <p class="meta">Whatever came out of the drum this week, as espresso or filter.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="card">
      <h2>Roast days</h2>
      <p class="meta">Tuesdays and Wednesdays. <a href="{{link 'roasting'}}">Watch a roast</a> if you cannot be here.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    <strong>One block of config.</strong> <code>router.js</code> declares the three things this
    site loads that store anything on a visitor's device: Google Tag Manager, a visit counter
    of the site's own, and YouTube. The banner, the settings dialog, the
    <a href="{{link 'cookies'}}">cookie table</a> and the gating on every page are made from it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Nothing loads until it is allowed.</strong> The layout's
    <code>consentScript</code> helpers write each script as
    <code>type="text/plain"</code>, which a browser does not run. When the visitor accepts a
    category, the script <code>consentHead</code> wrote turns those of that
    category back into scripts, in the order the page has them. The video on
    <a href="{{link 'roasting'}}">Watch a roast</a> waits for a click the same way.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Refusing is as easy as accepting.</strong> Accept all and Reject all are two buttons
    of one weight, with Choose cookies beside them for a choice in detail, and
    <em>Cookie settings</em> at the foot of every page reopens the choice.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
